1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
και in those cases, strange things can
happen.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
Suppose that are the sequence that
we're dealing with,

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:14.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
the A ij's take those particular
values indicated in this picture

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
and all the remaining terms, the A
ij's associated with the other dots, are all zeros,

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
so all these terms out there will be
zeros.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:34.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
If we carry out the summation by
fixing a j and adding over all i's,

7
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
what we get here is 0 and a 0 and a 0
and a 0.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
That's because in each row we have a 1
and a minus 1,

9
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
which cancel out and give us zeros.

10
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So if we carry out the summation in
this manner,

11
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:59.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
we get a sum of zeros, which is 0.

12
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:06.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
But if we carry out the summation in
this order, fix an i and then add over all j's.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
The first term that we get here is
going to be 1,

14
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
because in this column, this is the
only non-zero number,

15
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
and then in the remaining columns, as
we add the terms,

16
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
we're going to get zeros and zeros and
so on.

17
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
And so if we carry out the summation
in this way, we obtain a 1.

18
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So this is an example that shows you
that the order of summation actually may matter.

19
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:42.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
In this example, the sum of the
absolute values of all the terms that are involved is infinity,

20
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
because we have infinitely many plus
or minus ones.

21
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000 line:90% position:50% align:center region:bottom_center
So this condition here is not
satisfied.
